From 59fe54cc15408f84c2448c9efbe848a4d5a70201 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kayori <thatzokay@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 01:57:38 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] grate: layout v2: output on any row, working second tram
 link, copy propagation

The output write no longer sits on row 0. With the output class pinned
to row 0 the accumulator held R0-R2 for the whole program and no
round-start step could take a fetch (3 fetches per 4 steps on 4 rows).
NVIDIA puts the write on the last step on any row (its 5x5 blur ends on
row 2 of 3, accumulator in R10/R11). l2_schedule lets the output class
pick its row and puts the write on that step of the last round
(GRATE_LAYOUT_DWROW0=1 pins it to row 0 again). The blend stage follows:
create_blend_blob takes the row of the program's last output store,
puts the destination read and the output write on that step of the
extra round, and grate_fp_blend_alu(key, row, ...) moves its R0-R3
operands to the registers of that row.

The second tram link of 5c53372e2ca never happened for real programs:
emit_tgsi_input compared Declaration.Semantic, a one-bit "has semantic"
flag, with TGSI_SEMANTIC_COLOR, which is also 1, so every varying with a
semantic was excluded. It tests Semantic.Name now. color_input had the
same mistake, and held the declaration index although the flat shading
code indexes the link table by position.

Layout candidates: +reinterp-nodup lays the program out as if no row
were linked twice (it packs better for some programs, the 5x5 blur
among them). The +prop dimension was set and never read, so every
+prop candidate repeated its twin; it now propagates copies that cannot
be folded: the readers of a copy of a fetch result or of an
interpolation read the source itself, as NVIDIA's code does. A +prop
candidate is only generated when it propagated something. Compile time
of the 25-tap blur 6 s -> 3 s.

Checked on the tablet: glmark2 5x5 box blur 36 steps/35 packets to 28
steps/33 packets, a 1024x768 draw 52.5 to 49.7 ms (NVIDIA's compiled
program replayed in our driver: 33 steps/30 packets, 47.9 ms); N-tap
blurs of 3 to 25 taps are within 0 to 1.7 ms of NVIDIA's; glmark2's 49
programs 484 to 446 steps in total, none with more steps or packets
than before any of these changes; the 36 self-checking tests give the
same verdicts as the installed library; the new
tools/gpu/grate-blend-row-test (3x3 and 5x5 box, five blend modes,
output on row 2 of 3 and of 4) passes, also with a +prop layout forced;
grate-texture-lane-test passes with the second link active; no gr3d
reset. Costs measured in docs/grate-cost-model.md.
